# Day 1 - IP Address and DNS

## IP Address
An IP address identifies a device on a network and allows communication.

## DNS
DNS translates domain names into IP addresses.

## Questions

- How does DNS work?
DNS (Domain Name System) translates website names into IP addresses.

Example:
google.com → 142.x.x.x
Without DNS, we would have to remember IP addresses for every website.

Why it matters in cybersecurity:
Attackers often use malicious domains, so analysts investigate DNS activity to find suspicious communications.

- Why do websites have multiple IP addresses?
Large websites like Google have servers all over the world.

DNS can return different IP addresses depending on:

Your location
Server load
Availability

This helps with:

Faster performance
Load balancing
Reliability

Why it matters in cybersecurity:
Threat actors often change IP addresses while keeping the same domain, so analysts track both domains and IP infrastructure.

- How does HTTPS secure communication?
HTTPS secures communication between your browser and a website using TLS encryption.

Without HTTPS:
Data can be read if intercepted.

With HTTPS:
Data is encrypted.
The website proves its identity using a digital certificate.
Passwords and sensitive information are protected.

Why it matters in cybersecurity:
Analysts investigate phishing sites, certificates, and encrypted malware communications.

